{"parentUuid":"30b6f8a6-5e04-4ffc-8f5d-b60ed8ef9860","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZvQCZYc7p4GU1PHSM1L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All four checks passed: none of the items appear in gold or dev, are reprinted there, or are key-linked to them. I'm now writing POLICY v2, covering the rule changes, label record slices, schema values, and a dated change note.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3088,"cache_read_input_tokens":423377,"output_tokens":4326,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2241},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3088,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4326,"cache_read_input_tokens":423377,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3088,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3088},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":3,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"b0a41e1a-3959-4219-87dd-e29204d756cb","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgZvQBfE6zaG9mvvqhkqb","requestRef":"064c6c6e-4bd9-4afe-adca-bada857cc347","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"a6e72673-7e6a-40bf-9ee4-5f4b0cef0349","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:29:31.687Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"a6e72673-7e6a-40bf-9ee4-5f4b0cef0349","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZvQCZYc7p4GU1PHSM1L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GSnPcQqETHv7aPSuukwqM1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"eval/gold/POLICY.md\")\ns = p.read_text()\nreps = [\n# intro: v2 is dated, after the draw and before any label\n(\"\"\"These are the rules the gold set and the dev set are labelled by. This file was written and committed before the draw and before any label (decision 37). Every check of the reader rests on it.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"These are the rules the gold set and the dev set are labelled by. This file was written and committed before the draw and before any label (decision 37). Every check of the reader rests on it. Version 2, dated below, was committed after the draw and before any label.\n\"\"\"),\n# rules 6 and 9: the signals\n(\"\"\"     - Told as a tale, with a title, a narrator and literary framing, the same matter is `fiction` (rule 9).\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"     - Told as a tale, with a title, a narrator and literary framing, the same matter is `fiction` (rule 9).\n   - **Where rules 6 and 9 meet,** these are the signals:\n     - Printed as fact: a dateline, an attributed source, or a named witness.\n     - A tale: a literary title, a narrator's framing, extended dialogue, or a moral.\n     - Where they conflict, the text's own framing decides, and the note says so.\n\"\"\"),\n# rule 16: which item's fields\n(\"\"\"    - If any item is a report, the kind is `report`, and the fields come from that item.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"    - If any item is a report, the kind is `report`, and the fields come from that item. Where several items are reports, they come from the one that holds the lexicon's match, else the first.\n\"\"\"),\n# place_type: the schema's values\n(\"\"\"| `place_type` | For walks: `house`, `church`, `cemetery`, `inn or hotel`, `road or bridge`, `public building`, `ship` or `other`. |\"\"\",\n\"\"\"| `place_type` | For walks: `house`, `church`, `cemetery`, `inn_or_hotel`, `road_or_bridge`, `public_building`, `ship` or `other`, the schema's values. |\"\"\"),\n# rule 19, after rule 18\n(\"\"\"18. **Non-English text** follows the same rules, and its quotes stay in its own language.\n    - 1889: a French text whose match is figurative (`9_1889-08-01_p1_sn83045239_0029587661A_1889080101_0339.json`): `idiom`.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"18. **Non-English text** follows the same rules, and its quotes stay in its own language.\n    - 1889: a French text whose match is figurative (`9_1889-08-01_p1_sn83045239_0029587661A_1889080101_0339.json`): `idiom`.\n19. **Spirit-adjacent claims.**\n    - **Dreams and visions** in which a dead or dying person is claimed to appear or communicate, on a particular occasion, are a report. An ordinary or prophetic dream with no spirit claimed is `other`.\n    - **Omens and death warnings** told as having happened, and tied to a particular death or place, are a report: knocks, a banshee, a phantom funeral, a death light.\n      - A list of superstitions in general is `other`. 1853: a Greek burial custom for the spirit that lingers, with no manifestation (`2_1853-08-24_pna_sn85038115_00296027509_1853082401_0232.json`).\n      - So is an omen read into a natural event. 1913, Chester: a whale in Delaware Bay is taken for a precursor of war (`7_1913-07-29_p1_sn86069163_00202195854_1913072901_0312.json`).\n    - **Poltergeist doings** are a report: stones thrown, things moved, knockings, put down to a ghost or spirit or told as a haunting.\n    - **Clairvoyance, second sight, premonition or telepathy** with no spirit claimed is `other`, as in rule 3:\n      - 1900: Professor Barrett takes a dowser's success for clairvoyance (`17_1900-12-04_pna_sn89053684_00414182318_1900120401_1089.json`)\n      - 1902: a craze for crystal gazing in London (`5_1902-02-06_p1_sn84020751_00415667875_1902020601_0207.json`)\n    - **A spirit photograph** of a particular sitting or person is a report. Debate about spirit photography in general is `other`, as in rule 6: Dr. Seashore's 1922 rebuttal (rule 7's example).\n    - No judged item yet tells a dream of the dead, a death warning, a poltergeist or a spirit photograph, so those examples come from the other side of the line. Any example added later from the pool stays out of gold and dev.\n\"\"\"),\n# the label record: slices\n(\"\"\"- `rules`: the numbers of the rules above that were applied\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"- `rules`: the numbers of the rules above that were applied\n- `slices`: the edge-case slices of SRS 7.3 the article belongs to, from E1 to E14 but E9. E9, one story reprinted under different datelines, comes from the grouping rather than the label.\n\"\"\"),\n]\nfor old, new in reps:\n    assert s.count(old) == 1, old[:70]\n    s = s.replace(old, new)\ns = s.rstrip(\"\\n\") + \"\"\"\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Version 2, 2026-10-31,** committed after the draw (`984ae90`) and before any label, at the EM's call:\n  - rule 19, spirit-adjacent claims\n  - the signals where rules 6 and 9 meet\n  - which item gives a merged article's fields (rule 16)\n  - `place_type` in the schema's values\n  - the label record's `slices`\n- The split records version 1's sha256, `5bb4d07…27de`, the policy it was drawn under.\n\"\"\"\np.write_text(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"^19\\.\\|Where rules 6 and 9\\|inn_or_hotel\\|slices\\|## Changes\" eval/gold/POLICY.md","description":"Write POLICY v2's amendments"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3088,"cache_read_input_tokens":423377,"output_tokens":4326,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2241},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3088,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4326,"cache_read_input_tokens":423377,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3088,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3088},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GSnPcQqETHv7aPSuukwqM1":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"eval/gold/POLICY.md\")\ns = p.read_text()\nreps = [\n# intro: v2 is dated, after the draw and before any label\n(\"\"\"These are the rules the gold set and the dev set are labelled by. This file was written and committed before the draw and before any label (decision 37). Every check of the reader rests on it.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"These are the rules the gold set and the dev set are labelled by. This file was written and committed before the draw and before any label (decision 37). Every check of the reader rests on it. Version 2, dated below, was committed after the draw and before any label.\n\"\"\"),\n# rules 6 and 9: the signals\n(\"\"\"     - Told as a tale, with a title, a narrator and literary framing, the same matter is `fiction` (rule 9).\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"     - Told as a tale, with a title, a narrator and literary framing, the same matter is `fiction` (rule 9).\n   - **Where rules 6 and 9 meet,** these are the signals:\n     - Printed as fact: a dateline, an attributed source, or a named witness.\n     - A tale: a literary title, a narrator's framing, extended dialogue, or a moral.\n     - Where they conflict, the text's own framing decides, and the note says so.\n\"\"\"),\n# rule 16: which item's fields\n(\"\"\"    - If any item is a report, the kind is `report`, and the fields come from that item.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"    - If any item is a report, the kind is `report`, and the fields come from that item. Where several items are reports, they come from the one that holds the lexicon's match, else the first.\n\"\"\"),\n# place_type: the schema's values\n(\"\"\"| `place_type` | For walks: `house`, `church`, `cemetery`, `inn or hotel`, `road or bridge`, `public building`, `ship` or `other`. |\"\"\",\n\"\"\"| `place_type` | For walks: `house`, `church`, `cemetery`, `inn_or_hotel`, `road_or_bridge`, `public_building`, `ship` or `other`, the schema's values. |\"\"\"),\n# rule 19, after rule 18\n(\"\"\"18. **Non-English text** follows the same rules, and its quotes stay in its own language.\n    - 1889: a French text whose match is figurative (`9_1889-08-01_p1_sn83045239_0029587661A_1889080101_0339.json`): `idiom`.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"18. **Non-English text** follows the same rules, and its quotes stay in its own language.\n    - 1889: a French text whose match is figurative (`9_1889-08-01_p1_sn83045239_0029587661A_1889080101_0339.json`): `idiom`.\n19. **Spirit-adjacent claims.**\n    - **Dreams and visions** in which a dead or dying person is claimed to appear or communicate, on a particular occasion, are a report. An ordinary or prophetic dream with no spirit claimed is `other`.\n    - **Omens and death warnings** told as having happened, and tied to a particular death or place, are a report: knocks, a banshee, a phantom funeral, a death light.\n      - A list of superstitions in general is `other`. 1853: a Greek burial custom for the spirit that lingers, with no manifestation (`2_1853-08-24_pna_sn85038115_00296027509_1853082401_0232.json`).\n      - So is an omen read into a natural event. 1913, Chester: a whale in Delaware Bay is taken for a precursor of war (`7_1913-07-29_p1_sn86069163_00202195854_1913072901_0312.json`).\n    - **Poltergeist doings** are a report: stones thrown, things moved, knockings, put down to a ghost or spirit or told as a haunting.\n    - **Clairvoyance, second sight, premonition or telepathy** with no spirit claimed is `other`, as in rule 3:\n      - 1900: Professor Barrett takes a dowser's success for clairvoyance (`17_1900-12-04_pna_sn89053684_00414182318_1900120401_1089.json`)\n      - 1902: a craze for crystal gazing in London (`5_1902-02-06_p1_sn84020751_00415667875_1902020601_0207.json`)\n    - **A spirit photograph** of a particular sitting or person is a report. Debate about spirit photography in general is `other`, as in rule 6: Dr. Seashore's 1922 rebuttal (rule 7's example).\n    - No judged item yet tells a dream of the dead, a death warning, a poltergeist or a spirit photograph, so those examples come from the other side of the line. Any example added later from the pool stays out of gold and dev.\n\"\"\"),\n# the label record: slices\n(\"\"\"- `rules`: the numbers of the rules above that were applied\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"- `rules`: the numbers of the rules above that were applied\n- `slices`: the edge-case slices of SRS 7.3 the article belongs to, from E1 to E14 but E9. E9, one story reprinted under different datelines, comes from the grouping rather than the label.\n\"\"\"),\n]\nfor old, new in reps:\n    assert s.count(old) == 1, old[:70]\n    s = s.replace(old, new)\ns = s.rstrip(\"\\n\") + \"\"\"\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Version 2, 2026-10-31,** committed after the draw (`984ae90`) and before any label, at the EM's call:\n  - rule 19, spirit-adjacent claims\n  - the signals where rules 6 and 9 meet\n  - which item gives a merged article's fields (rule 16)\n  - `place_type` in the schema's values\n  - the label record's `slices`\n- The split records version 1's sha256, `5bb4d07…27de`, the policy it was drawn under.\n\"\"\"\np.write_text(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"^19\\.\\|Where rules 6 and 9\\|inn_or_hotel\\|slices\\|## Changes\" eval/gold/POLICY.md","description":"Write POLICY v2's amendments"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"b0a41e1a-3959-4219-87dd-e29204d756cb","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgZvQBfE6zaG9mvvqhkqb","requestRef":"06